The typed adapter and its recurrent pass count were frozen after the 22 execution questions. The subsequent tests change the requests or the comparator. In the two structural BFCL tests, success means completing a schema-valid call with the publisher-accepted arguments. No function is executed in those two tests. The catalog, live field and numerical execution endpoints also require a supported final answer.

#figure(image("typed-transfer.svg", width: 98%), caption: [One frozen two-pass tool checkpoint across paired comparisons. Circles represent the recurrent checkpoint and squares the named comparator. Horizontal intervals are marginal 95% Wilson intervals. The registered tests compare paired outcomes and use their discordant counts.])

On 24 requests with multiple competing functions, the frozen model completed 24 turns, generated 22 valid calls and produced 18 correct calls in 10.23 s. Base completed 15, generated nine valid calls and produced eight correct calls in 82.29 s. Twelve requests improved and two regressed (exact paired $p=0.01294$). These 65 function names were excluded from the training schemas and both previous public panels. Replaying the original single-function panel gave 16/24 versus 8/24 in 7.18 versus 95.46 s (eight gains, zero losses, $p=0.00781$). This latter result measures recovery on a known regression.

On the executable questions the recomputed two-pass model achieved 17/22 in 14.98 s. An ordinary-depth model trained from the same 8-bit instruction sibling on the same 1,074 turns, minibatches, 600 updates and 802,816 parameters achieved 18/22 in 14.37 s. The two-pass model lost one previously correct request and gained none. The advantage over untouched Base includes adaptation to the observation and call protocol. The equal-training experiment closed the depth attribution for this checkpoint.

In a separate execution optimization, 12 earlier multi-turn catalog requests were each repeated four times in balanced ABBA/BAAB order. Reusing aligned 128-token prefix state reduced combined elapsed time from 17.64 to 15.77 s. Every generated token, callback result and final answer was identical. Across the reuse runs, 5,120 prompt tokens reused previously computed state. The median within-request time ratio was 0.891. This experiment used the separately trained catalog checkpoint.


With the same frozen weights on the two older catalog panels together, the system scored 53/80 versus 12/80 for Base in 40.77 versus 170.62 s. There were 46 gains and five lost Base successes. It achieved 22/30 on the first panel but only 31/50 on the second, below the previously recorded 38/50. In the latter panel it answered two of ten direct numerical comparisons correctly, against ten for the separately trained four-layer model. The preregistered single-checkpoint retention gate failed.
